<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · site</p><h1 class="enc-title">Troy</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Ilion, Ilium, Hisarlik</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Troy, known to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greeks">Greeks</a> as Ilion, was an ancient city at Hisarlik in north-western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anatolia">Anatolia</a>, near the entrance to the Dardanelles. Inhabited from about 3000 BCE into the Roman period, it is famous as the setting of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trojan-war">Trojan War</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/homer">Homer</a>'s <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iliad">Iliad</a>, and its layers were excavated by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/heinrich-schliemann">Heinrich Schliemann</a> from 1870. It is a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco-world-heritage-site">UNESCO World Heritage Site</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/troy.jpg" alt="Troy" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: CherryX · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Walls_of_Troy_(2).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>The archaeological site of Troy consists of the hill of Hisarlık and the fields below it to the south. The hill is a tell, formed of strata holding the remains of more than three millennia of occupation. Nine main layers are identified, numbered with Roman numerals from Troy I, the earliest, to Troy IX, the latest, with sublayers marked by letters such as VIIa and VIIb. Relative dates come from comparing artefacts with those from other sites, and precise absolute dates are not always possible because <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/radiocarbon-dating">radiocarbon dating</a> has limited accuracy.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Troy I was founded around 3000 BCE beside a shallow lagoon and was notable for massive limestone fortifications. Built around 2550 BCE, Troy II was twice the size, with a lower town and a citadel divided into two precincts; its Megaron IIA is the biggest known building of its kind in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aegean">Aegean</a> and Anatolian region. Troy VI–VII was a major <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/late-bronze-age">Late Bronze Age</a> city with a steep citadel and a sprawling lower town, in contact with Mycenaean Greece. Hittite records name a city of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wilusa">Wilusa</a>, usually identified with it, though not all scholars regard the identification as firmly established. Sublayers VIh and VIIa show possible signs of violent destruction and are candidates for the setting of the later myths.</p><p>Troy VIII grew from about 700 BCE, when Greek immigrants began building, and lasted into the Roman era. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/herodotus">Herodotus</a> says that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xerxes-i">Xerxes</a> sacrificed 1,000 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cattle">cattle</a> at the sanctuary of Athena Ilias on his march towards <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greece">Greece</a>. Ilion was destroyed in 85 BCE after an eleven-day siege by the Roman general Fimbria, then rebuilt after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sulla">Sulla</a> bestowed benefactions on it, and its new civic calendar took 85 BCE as its first year. Earthquakes around 500 CE devastated the city, and from the 4th century until the Byzantine era Ilion was the seat of a Christian bishop.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Interest in the site began with travellers who mistook Alexandria Troas for Troy. Charles Maclaren was the first to identify Hisarlık with confidence, in 1822, and Frank Calvert surveyed it in 1865. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/heinrich-schliemann">Heinrich Schliemann</a> secured permission to excavate in 1868 and began his trench in April 1870; Wilhelm Dörpfeld, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carl-blegen">Carl Blegen</a> and, from 1988 to 2005, Manfred Korfmann continued the work, the last revealing the lower city. Schliemann proposed Troy II as the city of legend, though later research showed it predates the Mycenaean era by several hundred years. The relationship between myth and reality remains unclear. The Turkish government created a Historical National Park at Troy in 1996, and it became a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco-world-heritage-site">UNESCO World Heritage Site</a> in 1998.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Troy” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In the Iliad the Phrygians, 'the people of Otreus and godlike Mygdon', fight for <b>Troy</b> from a homeland on the Sangarius.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-phrygia">Kingdom of Phrygia · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Beyond politics, Thebes gave Greek literature the great Theban cycle of legend, the stories of Cadmus, Oedipus, the Seven against Thebes and the Epigoni, which rival the myths of <b>Troy</b> in their influence on classical writing.</blockquote><a href="/empire/thebes">Thebes · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>In legend the Trojan Aeneas, son of Venus, laid the basis of Roman religion when he brought the Palladium, the Lares and the Penates from <b>Troy</b>, and the Greek exile Evander set up the Greatest Altar of Hercules.</blockquote><a href="/religion/roman-religion">Roman religion · Origins</a></li><li><blockquote>Homer calls the Greeks who fought at <b>Troy</b> Achaeans, Danaans and Argives, and Hittite texts speak of a land called Ahhiyawa, a name most scholars connect with the Achaeans.</blockquote><a href="/culture/mycenaean-civilisation">Mycenaean civilisation · Origins</a></li><li><blockquote>As Greek settlements spread from the sixth century BCE, colonists tied their new homes to their existing myths, and the fall of <b>Troy</b> suited the purpose.</blockquote><a href="/work/aeneid">Aeneid · Origins</a></li><li><blockquote>He described Gelonus, in Scythia, as a city thousands of times larger than <b>Troy</b>, which was widely disbelieved until it was rediscovered in 1975.</blockquote><a href="/work/histories-of-herodotus">Histories of Herodotus · Survival &amp; Rediscovery</a></li></ul></section>
